>> It's one of those obscure shapes you usually find listed in obscure
>> geometry/topography textbooks right after mobius strips.  It's only a 
>> theoretical shape, and can't actually be created.  I think it's a bottle 
>> whose opening is inside the bottle, or something like that.
>
>It's quite analogous to a Moebius strip, actually. It's a closed surface
>whose "inside" coincides with its "outside". It can't be embedded in a
>three-dimensional Euclidean space (i.e. you can't build one in your back
>yard!) but it's quite easy to get a good approximation. Imagine a long,
>flexible cylinder. Punch a hole in its side, halfway down its length. Take
>one end of the cylinder, push it through the hole you made, and push it up
>so it just reaches the other end. The ends of the cylinder now form two
>concentric circles. Let them stick together, and you have a Klein bottle.
>
>The only difference between what you've got and the real thing is that a
>real Klein bottle doesn't have the hole you punched in the side.
>
>> PS: What's the word for the mathematical study of obscure shapes like
>>     that?
>
>Possibly the word you want is "topology". A Klein bottle is the simplest
>unbounded non-orientable surface. "Non-orientable" means that if you move
>a marking around on it, you can get it to come back to where it started as
>its own mirror-image, and I _think_ orientability is a topological property.

	Yes, it is.

